{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"PMC3954951","resultType":"core","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":25,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"24434237","source":"MED","pmid":"24434237","pmcid":"PMC3954951","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC3954951"]},"doi":"10.1177/0956797613513810","title":"The attentional blink reveals the probabilistic nature of discrete conscious perception.","authorString":"Asplund CL, Fougnie D, Zughni S, Martin JW, Marois R.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Asplund CL","firstName":"Christopher L","lastName":"Asplund","initials":"CL","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5708-6966"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"1Division of Social Sciences, Yale-NUS College."}]}},{"fullName":"Fougnie D","firstName":"Daryl","lastName":"Fougnie","initials":"D","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8245-883X"}},{"fullName":"Zughni S","firstName":"Samir","lastName":"Zughni","initials":"S"},{"fullName":"Martin JW","firstName":"Justin W","lastName":"Martin","initials":"JW","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6100-8581"}},{"fullName":"Marois R","firstName":"René","lastName":"Marois","initials":"R"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5708-6966"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6100-8581"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8245-883X"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"3","volume":"25","journalIssueId":2139009,"dateOfPublication":"2014 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2014,"printPublicationDate":"2014-03-01","journal":{"title":"Psychological science","medlineAbbreviation":"Psychol Sci","isoabbreviation":"Psychol Sci","nlmid":"9007542","essn":"1467-9280","issn":"0956-7976"}},"pubYear":"2014","pageInfo":"824-831","abstractText":"Attention and awareness are two tightly coupled processes that have been the subject of the same enduring debate: Are they allocated in a discrete or in a graded fashion? Using the attentional blink paradigm and mixture-modeling analysis, we show that awareness arises at central stages of information processing in an all-or-none manner. Manipulating the temporal delay between two targets affected subjects' likelihood of consciously perceiving the second target, but did not affect the precision of its representation. Furthermore, these results held across stimulus categories and paradigms, and they were dependent on attention having been allocated to the first target. The findings distinguish the fundamental contributions of attention and awareness at central stages of visual cognition: Conscious perception emerges in a quantal manner, with attention serving to modulate the probability that representations reach awareness.","affiliation":"1Division of Social Sciences, Yale-NUS College.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article","Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"P30-EY008126","agency":"NEI NIH HHS","acronym":"EY","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"R01 MH070776","agency":"NIMH NIH HHS","acronym":"MH","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"P30 EY008126","agency":"NEI NIH HHS","acronym":"EY","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"R01 MH70776","agency":"NIMH NIH HHS","acronym":"MH","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Probability"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Cognition"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Awareness"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Consciousness"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Psychomotor Performance"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adolescent"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adult"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Male"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Attentional Blink"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Young Adult"}]},"keywordList":{"key